# D46 — `==` on a type with NO `Equatable` implementation is a CHECK-TIME
# REJECTION naming `Equatable` and suggesting `@derive`.
#
#     enum Direction:
#         North()
#         South()
#     Direction.North() == Direction.North()      # -> error[E_UnsupportedOperator]
#
# This fixture GRADUATED out of `tests/fixtures/known_gaps/`. What it recorded
# there was the accept-then-answer-wrong behaviour: the comparison was accepted
# with no diagnostic and answered ADDRESS IDENTITY — `a == a` true, `a == b`
# false — identically on C, LLVM and ASan, while the definitional interpreter
# answered structurally. Three engines agreeing on a wrong answer is exactly
# what "matches the reference" cannot certify.
#
# Adding `@derive(Equatable)` still makes it correct, so the discriminator is
# the MISSING-IMPL path, not the comparison; structs behave identically. The
# rejection is at CHECK time, not eval time, so a comparison on a branch that
# never runs is refused too (`reject_eq_no_equatable_dead_branch.gg`).
#
# Asserted by `d46_eq_without_equatable_rejects` in tests/integration.rs.

enum Direction:
    North()
    South()

void main():
    Direction a = Direction.North()
    Direction b = Direction.North()
    if a == b:
        print("equal")
    else:
        print("NOT-equal")
